{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015Yoq9W5xmYdDow8BwERmF8","type":"tool_result","content":"commit 93699e6dc7da81c76e4b88f1cafa1b0f57cafa10\nAuthor: Patrick Devaney <patrickbdevaney@gmail.com>\nDate:   Thu Aug 20 10:23:50 2026 -0400\n\n    1.5: index_score as a register-tiled GEMM -- +4.57 % tok/s at ctx 12,410, and the context term\n    falls 0.572 +/- 0.018 ms per 1000, which is 89 % of what 0.4 predicted for this mark\n    \n    WHAT WAS MEASURED. One server load per arm, control (warp kernel) arm FIRST so drift favours the\n    control, four reps x three sweep contexts plus two control contexts:\n    \n      ctx    ms/forward before -> after   paired    tok/s before -> after    tau\n      3,197  142.94 -> 141.45             -1.49     12.14 -> 12.27 (+0.99 %) 1.736 both\n      6,260  146.95 -> 143.76             -3.19     11.87 -> 12.15 (+2.24 %) 1.788 both\n     12,410  154.44 -> 147.77             -6.67     10.46 -> 10.93 (+4.57 %) 1.615 both\n    \n    All 16 legs faster. tau, mean verify width and the EMITTED TEXT HASH identical in every one, at\n    ctx up to 12,410 -- not at ctx 9. Regressing the 16 paired deltas on context:\n    \n      delta_fwd = +0.358 +/- 0.134  -0.5724 +/- 0.0178 x (ctx/1000)    R^2 0.987, n=16\n    \n    WHAT IT MEANS. 0.4 attributed 0.644 +/- 0.018 ms per 1000 to the `i:score` mark. This delivered\n    0.572 +/- 0.018 -- prediction and delivery agree inside one SE, which is the strongest thing this\n    ladder has said about its own cost model. The tracked b goes 2.514 -> 1.942 (b x 6592: 16.57 ->\n    12.80 ms; stop wants <= 5.0), carried by SUBTRACTING a paired number rather than re-fitting a\n    narrow range, because over ctx 3,197-12,410 the arms fit b to R^2 0.701 and 0.433. Cumulatively b\n    is down 73 % from the 7.220 the ladder opened on. Note the intercept: at zero context the GEMM is\n    0.358 +/- 0.134 ms SLOWER, break-even near ctx 625 -- and on build/decode at seqmax 85 it is a\n    measured NULL (21.03-21.24 vs the warp kernel's 21.02-21.26, tau 2.87 in all six replicates,\n    generated ids byte-identical). A change worth +4.6 % where the kernel has rows and nothing where it\n    does not is behaving like a context-term change, which is the claim.\n    \n    THE MECHANISM, and the part worth remembering. The shipped warp kernel re-read both operands from\n    global on every head: 1.2 GB moved per call at the verify shape to do 151 M MACs, 0.5 FLOP/byte.\n    An intermediate tiled kernel fixes exactly that and is bit-identical to the shipped kernel -- and\n    that capped it at 2.0x, because bit-exactness with the warp kernel mandates keeping its 5-step\n    __shfl_down_sync tree, and SHFL is a ~200 us floor at this shape however the operands are staged.\n    The GEMM is bit-identical to index_score_kernel, the SCALAR REFERENCE gate_units checks against\n    ref/goldens, because register-tiled accumulation in k IS the reference's order -- and it is 6.8x.\n    \"Bit-exact\" is a two-place relation and the second argument is a design decision; claiming it\n    against the incumbent inherits every reassociation the incumbent ever made, including (Finding 68)","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8210199f-4413-4daf-85d7-08e4fdccb06e","uuid":"da7840ab-09f9-45b9-a4b0-275ff6755ca9","timestamp":"2026-08-20T16:46:26.030Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"commit 93699e6dc7da81c76e4b88f1cafa1b0f57cafa10\nAuthor: Patrick Devaney <patrickbdevaney@gmail.com>\nDate:   Thu Aug 20 10:23:50 2026 -0400\n\n    1.5: index_score as a register-tiled GEMM -- +4.57 % tok/s at ctx 12,410, and the context term\n    falls 0.572 +/- 0.018 ms per 1000, which is 89 % of what 0.4 predicted for this mark\n    \n    WHAT WAS MEASURED. One server load per arm, control (warp kernel) arm FIRST so drift favours the\n    control, four reps x three sweep contexts plus two control contexts:\n    \n      ctx    ms/forward before -> after   paired    tok/s bef